<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
    Lint configuration for the whole Android tree.

    AGP collects every lint.xml from the module directory up to the root
    project directory, so this file applies to :app as well.

    ---------------------------------------------------------------------
    1. PropertyEscape - silenced FOR ONE FILE, not for the whole project
    ---------------------------------------------------------------------
    android/local.properties is machine-local and gitignored. It holds the
    path of the developer's Android SDK, and the way that path is spelled
    differs per machine and per tool (Android Studio writes
    "C\:\\Users\\me\\Android\\Sdk", a hand-written file usually says
    "C:/Users/me/Android/Sdk"). PropertyEscape is right about the format -
    an unescaped backslash or drive letter colon really is mis-parsed by
    java.util.Properties - but a check that fails the build because of a
    file that is not under version control fails on every developer who
    does not have the same SDK path.

    The path matches the FILE NAME, not one machine's directory: the report
    names the ASCII junction the build was started from
    ("C:\Temp\nedotify-<id>\project\local.properties") and a CI runner names
    its checkout. Every other file of the tree stays checked -
    android/gradle.properties among them. A global `severity="ignore"`
    would have silenced the check for the repository's own property files
    as well, and the report of the area claimed a per-file suppression that
    the file did not contain.

    ---------------------------------------------------------------------
    2. The three "a newer version is available" detectors
    ---------------------------------------------------------------------
    NewerVersionAvailable, GradleDependency and AndroidGradlePluginVersion
    read a repository over the network and cache the answer in
    app/build/intermediates/lint-cache/lintAnalyzeDebug/maven-versions/.
    The audit measured 15, 13 and 4 warnings for the SAME source, depending
    on the state of that cache and of the network: a gate whose result
    depends on the network is not a gate, and a clean checkout produced a
    different warning set than a warm one.

    `lint { checkDependencies = false }` (android/build.gradle) does NOT
    remove them - measured on AGP 9.1.1: with the flag false the lint model
    is regenerated and the three detectors still report (17 warnings, the
    same as with the flag on). They are therefore switched off HERE, and the
    information is produced by the separate, NON-BLOCKING task
    `:reportNewerVersions`, which never fails the build.

    Every other check stays enabled, and lint still aborts on an error
    (android/build.gradle: lint { abortOnError = true; checkDependencies = false }).
-->
<lint>
    <issue id="PropertyEscape">
        <ignore path="local.properties" />
    </issue>
    <issue id="NewerVersionAvailable" severity="ignore" />
    <issue id="GradleDependency" severity="ignore" />
    <issue id="AndroidGradlePluginVersion" severity="ignore" />
</lint>
